b_text:
• Create an array of size 100
• Use student ids as keys
Student IDs: 11,000, 11,001, …, 11,099
Hash Function:
int hash(int key) {
return key - 11000;
}
int hash(int key) {
return key % 11000;
}  
Ideal Hashing
• These are example of perfect hash functions: every
possible key value corresponds to a single index location
• Insert operation:
void insert(K key, V value) {
array[hash(key)] = value;
}  
Ideal Hashing
• These are example of perfect hash functions: every
possible key value corresponds to a single index location
• Lookup operation:
V lookup(K key) {
return array[hash(key)];
}  
Ideal Hashing
• These are example of perfect hash functions: every
possible key value corresponds to a single index location
• Deletion operation:
void remove(K key) {
array[hash(key)] = null;
}  
The real world is not ideal
• Our real world UW-Madison student IDs are 10 digit
numbers:
9021453190, 9033879101, 9024357190
• To define a perfect hash function we would need to define
it over the space from 0 to 10 billion
• This already exceeds the maximum size of a Java array
which is around 2 billion, so we can’t have a unique index  
The real world is not ideal
• Our real world UW-Madison student IDs are 10 digit
numbers:
9021453190, 9033879101, 9024357190
• What if we just use the last three digits of the student id?
• We will have a collision: multiple keys corresponding to
the same index provided by our hash function  
